# R47 Track B — INDIRECT-DISPATCH CALL RESULT NEVER REGISTERED FOR DROP,
# MEASURED ON THE REPETITION AXIS.
#
# CELL: arm = `Box[Trait]` vtable dispatch · position = DISCARDED TEMP inside
# a LOOP · payload = String · 5 iterations.
#
# ⚠ THE RESULT MUST NOT BE BOUND TO A NAMED LOCAL. Writing
# `String s = b.speak()` inside the loop makes this fixture GREEN at pristine
# HEAD: the named-local binding takes ownership downstream and rescues the
# unregistered temp. That rescue is exactly why three earlier censuses read
# this class as "unnamed temps leak" and missed the real discriminator. The
# loop body therefore consumes the result as a temp.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS CELL EXISTS SEPARATELY. The single-call cells make the defect
# look like fixed-size resource debt. It is not: the result is minted fresh on
# EVERY call, so the leak scales with iteration count — any loop calling a
# trait-object method leaks O(iterations). This fixture pins the unbounded
# character, and a partial regression that re-leaks only the loop shape trips
# here and nowhere else.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `security_safe_no_leak`, C lane only (ASan is a no-op under
# `--backend=llvm`).
#
# RED-VERIFIED at pristine `f3feea79`: LEAK 20 byte(s) / 5 allocations, 3/3
# runs — five allocations for five iterations, which is the point.
from std.collections import Box

trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)

struct Robot:
    String id

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id + "!"

void main():
    Box[Speaker] b = Box.new(Robot("R2"))
    int n = 0
    for i in 0..5:
        n = n + b.speak().len()
    print(n)
